---
import Research from '../../layouts/Research.astro';
import ImagingPlayer from '../../components/ImagingPlayer.astro';
import CA1Layers from '../../components/CA1Layers.astro';
import Label from '../../components/Label.astro';
import TwoPhotonScope from '../../components/TwoPhotonScope.astro';
import OcularDive from '../../components/OcularDive.astro';
import GraphicalAbstract from '../../components/GraphicalAbstract.astro';
import DataPipeline from '../../components/DataPipeline.astro';
---
<Research theme="two-photon" color="var(--green)" eyebrow="Research · 01" title="Two-photon imaging in hippocampal CA1"
  lead="Watching identified neurons in the living hippocampus — cell by cell, day after day.">
  <Fragment slot="question">
    <h2>How does β-amyloid make hippocampal neurons hyperactive — and can it be stopped early?</h2>
    <p>Neuronal hyperactivity is one of the earliest signs of circuit dysfunction in Alzheimer’s disease, appearing before amyloid plaques. We image hundreds of CA1 neurons simultaneously to see which cells become hyperactive, why, and whether interventions can restore normal activity.</p>
    <p class="small muted">Draft based on Zott et al. (2019, 2024) — please review wording.</p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="method">
    <div class="grid grid-2" style="align-items:center">
      <div>
        <h2>Why two photons?</h2>
        <p>A two-photon microscope uses pulsed infrared light. Fluorescence is only excited where two photons arrive at the same time — practically only in the tiny focal spot. This gives sharp optical sections deep in scattering tissue with less photodamage.</p>
        <p class="muted">With genetically encoded calcium indicators such as GCaMP, neurons light up when they are active, so the activity of hundreds of cells can be followed simultaneously.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 400 260" role="img" aria-labelledby="tp-t">
          <title id="tp-t">One-photon excitation produces fluorescence along the whole light cone; two-photon excitation only at the focal point.</title>
          <g font-family="JetBrains Mono Variable, monospace" font-size="11" fill="var(--text-3)">
            <text x="100" y="20" text-anchor="middle">one-photon</text><text x="300" y="20" text-anchor="middle">two-photon</text>
          </g>
          <path d="M60 40 L140 40 L100 150 Z M100 150 L60 250 L140 250 Z" fill="var(--blue)" opacity=".18" />
          <path d="M60 40 L140 40 L100 150 Z M100 150 L60 250 L140 250 Z" fill="var(--green)" opacity=".35" />
          <path d="M260 40 L340 40 L300 150 Z M300 150 L260 250 L340 250 Z" fill="#c0392b" opacity=".18" />
          <circle cx="300" cy="150" r="7" fill="var(--green)" />
          <circle cx="300" cy="150" r="16" fill="var(--green)" opacity=".25" />
        </svg>
        <figcaption><Label kind="schematic" /> <span>Excitation volume (green): one- vs two-photon.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <h3 style="margin-top:var(--s6)">How the image is built</h3>
    <p class="muted" style="max-width:var(--measure)">The microscope never takes a snapshot. Two scan mirrors sweep the focal spot across the field line by line, and a detector counts the green light coming back from each point. The picture is assembled from those counts.</p>
    <div style="margin-top:var(--s4)"><TwoPhotonScope /></div>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="data">
    <div class="bleed">
      <OcularDive label="Illustration · neurons firing" steps={['Look through the eyepiece.', 'Inside the living brain, neurons are firing.', 'Each flash is one neuron becoming active. Here is what that looks like in real data.']} />
    </div>
    <h2 data-reveal style="margin-top:var(--s6)">Watch neurons fire</h2>
    <p class="muted" data-reveal>In vivo recordings of CA1 pyramidal neurons from three mouse lines. Switch between recordings or compare them side by side, step through frames, and change the display colour table. Neurons flash briefly when they are active.</p>
    <div style="margin-top:var(--s4)"><ImagingPlayer /></div>
    <h3 style="margin-top:var(--s6)">Where we image</h3>
    <CA1Layers />
    <h2 data-reveal style="margin-top:var(--s7)">From pixels to activity traces</h2>
    <p class="muted" data-reveal style="max-width:var(--measure)">Scroll on to follow one of our recordings through the analysis: each neuron is outlined as a region of interest, its brightness over time becomes a ΔF/F trace, and every peak marks a calcium transient.</p>
    <div class="bleed"><DataPipeline /></div>
    <h2 data-reveal style="margin-top:var(--s7)">The story behind the data</h2>
    <p class="muted" data-reveal style="max-width:var(--measure)">What the hyperactive neurons in these recordings tell us about how Alzheimer’s disease begins, step by step.</p>
    <div class="bleed"><GraphicalAbstract /></div>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="findings">
    <ul class="findings">
      <li><strong>Hyperactivity feeds itself.</strong> β-amyloid suppresses glutamate reuptake, so glutamate accumulates at synapses and drives neurons that are already active into hyperactivity — a vicious cycle that can start before plaques form. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aay0198">Zott et al., Science 2019</a></li>
      <li><strong>Scavenging Aβ monomers prevents it.</strong> An anticalin protein that binds β-amyloid monomers suppressed early neuronal hyperactivity and synaptic glutamate accumulation in young APP23xPS45 mice. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-50153-y">Zott et al., Nat Commun 2024</a></li>
      <li><strong>Timing matters.</strong> In old, plaque-bearing APP23xPS45 mice (19 % of 237 CA1 neurons hyperactive), the anticalin and the γ-secretase inhibitor Ly-411575 no longer reduced hyperactivity — pointing to an early therapeutic window. <span class="muted">(Nat Commun 2024, Suppl. Fig. S13)</span></li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</Research>
<script>
  // The scroll stories load when the visitor gets close (same modules as on the homepage)
  import { ScrollTrigger } from 'gsap/ScrollTrigger';
  const lazy = (sel: string, load: (el: HTMLElement) => void) => {
    const el = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(sel);
    if (!el) return;
    const io = new IntersectionObserver(([e]) => { if (e.isIntersecting) { io.disconnect(); load(el); } }, { rootMargin: '150% 0px' });
    io.observe(el);
  };
  lazy('[data-dp]', (el) => import('../../scripts/pipeline').then((m) => { m.initPipeline(el); ScrollTrigger.refresh(); }));
  lazy('[data-ga]', (el) => import('../../scripts/abstract').then((m) => { m.initAbstract(el); ScrollTrigger.refresh(); }));
</script>
<style>
  .bleed { width: 100vw; margin-left: calc(50% - 50vw); margin-top: var(--s5); }
  .findings { display: grid; gap: var(--s3); padding-left: 1.1em; } .findings li { max-width: var(--measure); } .findings a { white-space: nowrap; }</style>
